  "steps": [
    {
      "place": "sisupalgarh",
      "phase": "before",
      "title": "The land of Kalinga",
      "native": "କଳିଙ୍ଗ",
      "when": "before the 3rd century BCE",
      "at": 2,
      "source": "History",
      "camera": {
        "at": [
          84.8,
          19.8
        ],
        "width": 10
      },
      "legs": [],
      "text": "Kalinga is the coastal land between the Mahanadi and Godavari rivers, in today’s Odisha and northern Andhra Pradesh. The Mahabharata names its kings, but it is not among the sixteen great kingdoms of Buddhist lists. Near Bhubaneswar lie the walls of Sisupalgarh, a large square city that may be its capital, Tosali, or Kalinganagari; the identification is debated.",
      "today": "Sisupalgarh’s earthen ramparts and gateways still stand on the eastern edge of Bhubaneswar."
    },
    {
      "place": "pataliputra",
      "phase": "before",
      "title": "A neighbour Magadha wanted",
      "native": "पाटलिपुत्र",
      "lang": "hi",
      "when": "4th to 3rd century BCE",
      "at": 10,
      "source": "History",
      "camera": {
        "at": [
          85.2,
          24.5
        ],
        "width": 10
      },
      "legs": [],
      "text": "Kharavela’s later inscription says a Nanda king (Nandaraja) once opened a canal in Kalinga and carried off its Jain image, so the Nandas probably ruled it. By the Mauryas’ time Kalinga was free. Pliny, probably drawing on Megasthenes at Pataliputra, says its king kept 60,000 foot soldiers, 1,000 horsemen and 700 elephants.",
      "today": "Patna stands on Pataliputra; parts of its timber walls and a Maurya pillared hall have been excavated."
    },
    {
      "place": "dhauli",
      "phase": "war",
      "title": "Ashoka marches south",
      "when": "about 261 BCE",
      "at": 18,
      "source": "Archaeology",
      "camera": {
        "at": [
          85.4,
          22.9
        ],
        "width": 12
      },
      "legs": [
        {
          "from": "pataliputra",
          "to": "dhauli",
          "traveller": "maurya-army",
          "style": "army",
          "bend": 0.1
        }
      ],
      "text": "Ashoka’s Thirteenth Rock Edict dates the war: Kalinga was conquered when he had been consecrated eight years, about 261 BCE (some scholars say 262 or 260). No source says why he attacked. Historians suggest Kalinga sat across the routes to the south and the sea, and its army was strong, but that is a guess.",
      "today": "Trains from Patna to Bhubaneswar take most of a day along the route an army walked."
    },
    {
      "place": "dhauli",
      "phase": "war",
      "title": "The battle by the Daya",
      "native": "ଦୟା ନଦୀ",
      "when": "about 261 BCE",
      "at": 26,
      "source": "Tradition",
      "camera": {
        "at": [
          85.2,
          19.9
        ],
        "width": 9
      },
      "legs": [],
      "text": "No ancient source names the battlefield. Local tradition places the decisive fight on the plain below Dhauli hill, beside the Daya river, and says the river ran red with blood. Ashoka’s edicts at Dhauli suggest it mattered to him, but they say nothing of a battle there. The king of Kalinga is not named anywhere.",
      "today": "The Daya still flows past Dhauli hill; a festival, Dhauli Mahotsav, is held there each year."
    },
    {
      "place": "kalsi",
      "phase": "dhamma",
      "title": "The Thirteenth Edict",
      "native": "तेरहवाँ शिलालेख",
      "lang": "hi",
      "when": "about 256 BCE",
      "at": 34,
      "source": "Archaeology",
      "camera": {
        "at": [
          78.5,
          29.5
        ],
        "width": 12
      },
      "legs": [],
      "text": "On rocks across the empire, as at Kalsi in the Himalayan foothills, Ashoka confesses: 150,000 people were carried away from Kalinga, 100,000 were killed, and many times that number died. These are his figures; historians debate them. He writes of deep sorrow, and says even a hundredth or thousandth of that suffering would now grieve him.",
      "today": "The Kalsi rock, with all fourteen major edicts, stands under a shelter near the Yamuna."
    },
    {
      "place": "kandahar",
      "phase": "dhamma",
      "title": "Conquest by dhamma",
      "native": "Πιοδάσσης",
      "lang": "grc",
      "when": "about 256 BCE",
      "at": 42,
      "source": "Archaeology",
      "camera": {
        "at": [
          66.8,
          31.2
        ],
        "width": 12
      },
      "legs": [],
      "text": "Part of the same edict, in Greek, was found at Kandahar in 1963. The edict goes on to call conquest by moral law (dhamma) the best conquest, and claims it has reached five Greek kings: Antiochus, Ptolemy, Antigonus, Magas and Alexander. It also warns the forest peoples that the repentant king still has power to punish.",
      "today": "Kandahar’s old city, where the inscription was found, lies in ruins west of the modern town."
    },
    {
      "place": "dhauli",
      "phase": "dhamma",
      "title": "Silence in Kalinga",
      "native": "ଧଉଳି",
      "when": "about 256 BCE",
      "at": 50,
      "source": "Archaeology",
      "camera": {
        "at": [
          85.2,
          20.2
        ],
        "width": 9
      },
      "legs": [],
      "text": "At Dhauli, in Kalinga itself, the confession is missing. The edicts here leave out the Thirteenth and add two special edicts for the officers (mahamatras) of Tosali. “All men are my children,” the first says, and it orders fair justice and no needless imprisonment or torture. Above the inscription, an elephant is carved from the rock.",
      "today": "The edicts and the rock-cut elephant can still be seen on Dhauli hill, under a protective roof."
    },
    {
      "place": "jaugada",
      "phase": "dhamma",
      "title": "“Do not fear me”",
      "native": "ଜଉଗଡ଼",
      "when": "about 256 BCE",
      "at": 58,
      "source": "Archaeology",
      "camera": {
        "at": [
          84.5,
          19.6
        ],
        "width": 9
      },
      "legs": [],
      "text": "At Jaugada, a walled town further south, the same special edicts are addressed to the officers of Samapa. The second speaks to the peoples beyond his borders who are not yet conquered: the king wishes that they should not fear him, but trust him. Officers are to be inspected every five years to keep them honest.",
      "today": "Jaugada’s fort is a mound of earth by the Rushikulya river; the inscribed rock is protected on site."
    },
    {
      "place": "sarnath",
      "phase": "dhamma",
      "title": "A king turns to the Buddha",
      "native": "सारनाथ",
      "lang": "hi",
      "when": "about 260 to 250 BCE",
      "at": 66,
      "source": "Archaeology",
      "camera": {
        "at": [
          83.5,
          25.4
        ],
        "width": 10
      },
      "legs": [],
      "text": "After Kalinga, says the Thirteenth Edict, came Ashoka’s zeal for dhamma. A minor edict adds that he had been a lay Buddhist (upasaka) for over two and a half years before drawing close to the monastic order. Later Buddhist chronicles tell other conversion stories and never mention Kalinga. His pillar at Sarnath warns monks against splitting the order.",
      "today": "Sarnath’s lion capital, from Ashoka’s pillar, is India’s national emblem."
    },
    {
      "place": "udayagiri",
      "phase": "kharavela",
      "title": "Kharavela of Kalinga",
      "native": "ଖାରବେଳ",
      "when": "2nd or 1st century BCE",
      "at": 74,
      "source": "Archaeology",
      "camera": {
        "at": [
          85.3,
          20.2
        ],
        "width": 9
      },
      "legs": [],
      "text": "After the Mauryas fall, Kalinga is free again. Its Jain king Kharavela, of the Chedi (Mahameghavahana) line, carves his reign year by year above the Elephant Cave (Hathigumpha) at Udayagiri. He repairs his capital after a storm, sends an army west in defiance of the Satavahana king Satakarni, and has caves cut for Jain monks. His dates are disputed.",
      "today": "The Udayagiri and Khandagiri caves, on Bhubaneswar’s western edge, are open to visitors."
    },
    {
      "place": "pataliputra",
      "phase": "kharavela",
      "title": "Kalinga strikes back",
      "native": "पाटलिपुत्र",
      "lang": "hi",
      "when": "2nd or 1st century BCE",
      "at": 82,
      "source": "Archaeology",
      "camera": {
        "at": [
          85.4,
          22.9
        ],
        "width": 12
      },
      "legs": [
        {
          "from": "udayagiri",
          "to": "pataliputra",
          "traveller": "kalinga-army",
          "style": "army",
          "bend": 0.1
        }
      ],
      "text": "Kharavela’s inscription says he attacked Rajagriha, then made a king of Magadha bow at his feet. The name is read as Bahasatimita, though the reading is disputed. He brings home the Jain image of Kalinga (Kalinga Jina) that a Nanda king had carried off. Two or three centuries after Ashoka, Kalinga has turned the tables.",
      "today": "Rajgir, the old Rajagriha, lies about 100 km south-east of Patna among five hills."
    },
    {
      "place": "dhauli",
      "phase": "remembered",
      "title": "The edicts read again",
      "when": "1837 to 1838 CE",
      "at": 90,
      "source": "History",
      "camera": {
        "at": [
          85.2,
          20.2
        ],
        "width": 9
      },
      "legs": [],
      "text": "For two thousand years no one could read Ashoka’s script. In 1837 Markham Kittoe copied the Dhauli inscriptions, and James Prinsep, who had just deciphered the Brahmi script, published them. The confession about Kalinga was read later, from copies elsewhere such as Shahbazgarhi and Kalsi. Why Ashoka left it out in Kalinga is still debated.",
      "today": "Brahmi, the script Prinsep read, is the ancestor of most scripts of South and Southeast Asia, Odia included."
    },
    {
      "place": "dhauli",
      "phase": "remembered",
      "title": "A pagoda for peace",
      "native": "ଶାନ୍ତି ସ୍ତୂପ",
      "when": "1972 CE to today",
      "at": 98,
      "source": "History",
      "camera": {
        "at": [
          85.2,
          20.2
        ],
        "width": 9
      },
      "legs": [],
      "text": "In 1972 a white peace pagoda (Shanti Stupa) was built on Dhauli hill with help from Japanese Buddhists, looking over the plain where tradition sets the battle. Odisha still calls itself the land of Kalinga. The war is remembered less for the conquest than for what Ashoka said afterwards: that he regretted it.",
      "today": "The Shanti Stupa crowns Dhauli hill, a few kilometres south of Bhubaneswar."
    }
  ],
